{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The presence of letters appearing on the INN stem list does not by itself establish a section 43 objection under the Manual: the examiner must assess whether their inclusion is meaningful enough to convey a connotation likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1",
          "quote": "An examiner will need to consider whether the presence of an INN stem in a word is “meaningful” enough to give rise to a connotation leading to likely deception or confusion under section 43. Some INN stems consist of simple two or three letter combinations and their presence in a larger word may not be meaningful and as a result would not lead to deception or confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Section 43 requires rejection where a connotation of the mark, or a sign it contains, makes use for the particular goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Check the stem's designated position: the Manual explains that hyphens indicate whether it is a prefix, suffix or infix.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/2",
          "quote": "Stems can be prefixes, suffixes or infixes - this is denoted by the position and presence of hyphens in the list of INN stems. For example: -PROFEN is a suffix CEF- is a prefix ESTR is an infix that could appear anywhere in a word (including at the start or end) -KEF- is an infix that should appear within the word."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Assess the letters in the context of the whole mark, including competing references and whether the whole mark's meaning overwhelms the stem.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1",
          "quote": "For example, the presence of ‘aj’ in the term ‘Sansajabendorastine’ is not significant enough to warrant a ground for rejection given the length of the name and other competing references such as ‘astine’."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1",
          "quote": "In addition, a stem may not be included meaningfully in a trade mark where its presence is clearly overwhelmed by the meaning of the trade mark as a whole."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual also records factors pointing against a section 43 objection where most are present: common non-INN use of the suffix, a two- or three-letter stem, alternative obvious suffixes, and non-specificity in the context of the mark.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1",
          "quote": "Where most of the following factors are present: a. The suffix is in common use other than in its INN-stem connotation, as evidenced by both the: i. state of the Register; and ii. the marketplace b. The INN-stem is two or three letters long, c. There are other or alternative obvious suffixes present in the trade mark."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/6~1",
          "quote": "d. The INN-stem is non-specific - that is, in the context of the trade mark under consideration the INN-stem would not be generally apprehended as indicating only a particular kind of pharmaceutical because the ‘prefix’ does not conform with the usual formulation specific to the INN-stem under consideration, The section 43 ground should not be raised as it is unlikely that the use of the INN-stem would deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual, a section 43 ground exists for pharmaceuticals or veterinary substances where the mark consists of an INN stem or meaningfully contains a notified INN stem, and the specification does not restrict the goods to substances indicated by that stem.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/5",
          "quote": "A ground for rejection under section 43 exists where a trade mark to be used in relation to pharmaceuticals or veterinary substances consists of an INN stem, or contains a notified INN stem in a meaningful way, and use of the trade mark in respect of the goods covered by the specification are not restricted to substances indicated by the INN stem."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual says that this section 43 ground may be overcome by an agreed condition of registration limiting use to goods containing substances belonging to the relevant INN stem.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/5/5/5",
          "quote": "A section 43 ground for rejection may be overcome by the applicant agreeing to a condition of registration limiting use of the trade mark to goods containing substances belonging to the relevant INN stem."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Separately, the Manual treats a section 41 objection as appropriate where the entire mark for pharmaceutical or veterinary substances is identical to a notified INN stem, and potentially appropriate for substantially identical or, rarely, confusingly similar marks.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part22/24/24/2",
          "quote": "A ground for rejection under section 41 is appropriate where a trade mark to be used in relation to pharmaceutical or veterinary substances, is in its entirety identical to a notified INN stem."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part22/24/24/2",
          "quote": "In some cases, it may also be appropriate to take a ground for rejection in relation to trade marks which are substantially identical (or in rare instances, confusingly similar) to a notified INN stem."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "These passages provide an assessment framework, not an outcome for the unspecified mark; without its wording and relevant goods, whether the inclusion is meaningful remains uncertain.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
